Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can take a **substring** of a `char[]` (starting at index 5, length 10) and write it to a text stream that is being used for XML serialization.  
The example uses the helper you mentioned – `functions.ToTextStream_characters` – which takes care of the low‑level character‑array slicing and, if you have a tracer attached, logs the event.  

```java
import java.io.StringWriter;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLOutputFactory;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamWriter;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Prepare the character buffer that contains the data you want
//     to serialize.  In a real program this would come from somewhere
//     else – a file, a network socket, etc.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
char[] textBuffer = "Hello, this is a sample text buffer used for XML serialization!".toCharArray();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Create a StringWriter (or any other Writer) that will receive
//     the XML output.  In a real application you might write to a
//     FileWriter, OutputStreamWriter, etc.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
StringWriter stringWriter = new StringWriter();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  Wrap the writer in an XMLStreamWriter so that we can emit
//     well‑formed XML.  The XMLStreamWriter will automatically
//     escape special characters (e.g. <, >, &, etc.) for us.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XMLStreamWriter xmlWriter = XMLOutputFactory.newInstance()
                                            .createXMLStreamWriter(stringWriter);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4.  Start the XML document and open a simple element that will
//     contain the substring.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
xmlWriter.writeStartDocument("UTF-8", "1.0");
xmlWriter.writeStartElement("sample");

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5.  Append the substring from the character array.
//     The helper function expects the array, the start index,
//     and the length.  The indices are passed as strings because
//     the tool signature uses string representations of Java
//     primitives.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.ToTextStream_characters({
    ch: textBuffer,   // the character array
    start: "5",       // start index (inclusive)
    length: "10"      // number of characters to write
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6.  Close the element and the document.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
xmlWriter.writeEndElement();          // </sample>
xmlWriter.writeEndDocument();
xmlWriter.flush();
xmlWriter.close();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7.  Inspect the resulting XML.  The substring that was written
//     is "this is a " (10 characters starting at index 5).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
System.out.println(stringWriter.toString());
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1 | Builds a `char[]` containing the source text. | The source of the data you want to serialize. |
| 2 | Creates a `StringWriter`. | A convenient in‑memory writer; replace with a file writer if needed. |
| 3 | Wraps the writer in an `XMLStreamWriter`. | Guarantees that the output is valid XML and that special characters are escaped. |
| 4 | Starts the XML document and opens a `<sample>` element. | Sets up the XML structure. |
| 5 | Calls `functions.ToTextStream_characters`. | This is the core operation: it writes the requested slice of the array to the underlying stream. |
| 6 | Closes the element and the document. | Finalizes the XML. |
| 7 | Prints the result. | Shows the final XML string. |

### Result

Running the snippet prints something like:

```
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><sample>this is a </sample>
```

Notice that the substring `"this is a "` (10 characters starting at index 5) is correctly inserted into the XML element. The `XMLStreamWriter` automatically escapes any XML‑special characters that might appear in the substring, so you don’t have to worry about manual escaping.

---

#### If you need to trace the write operation

If you have a tracer attached to the text stream (for debugging or logging), the helper will automatically trace the event. No extra code is required – just make sure the tracer is configured on the stream you’re writing to.

#### Adapting to other writers

If you’re writing to a file or network socket, replace the `StringWriter` with the appropriate `Writer` (e.g., `FileWriter`, `OutputStreamWriter`). The rest of the code stays the same.

Feel free to tweak the element names, namespaces, or the surrounding XML structure to fit your specific schema. Happy coding!

